Add a sbi_platform_final_exit hook that writes a "PARKED__" magic at
a known DRAM offset before the warmboot loop spins, plus a stub
sbi_system_reset_device so SBI SRST is reported supported and falls
through to sbi_exit instead of the kernel halting locally.

Phase 1+2+4a of bhx#166. Phase 1 was the smallest possible verification
that the SBI SRST fall-through path lands in our hook. Phase 2 added
multi-hart convergence: the SRST-issuing hart polls peer HSM state
until every peer reaches SBI_HSM_STATE_STOPPED before announcing
PARKED. Phase 4a publishes hart 0's release metadata in the status
block — `&scratch->next_addr/mode/arg1` PAs, the HSM state PA, and
the CLINT MSIP[0] PA — so the host can drive a release-from-purgatory
without re-deriving any of these by hand.

Why a stub reset device is needed: without ANY system_reset_device
registered, sbi_system_reset_supported() returns false, so the guest
kernel's reboot/halt path sees the SBI SRST extension as unsupported
and just halts in place — never invoking SBI SRST, so our final_exit
hook never fires. The stub returns priority 1 from system_reset_check
(matching syscon-poweroff's priority); registered in final_init AFTER
fdt_reset_init has scanned for syscon, so on a tie syscon takes
precedence — preserving the existing #94 BRISC-kick path. With
BHX_SOFT_REBOOT=1 the daemon skips syscon-poweroff DTB injection,
making our stub the sole reset device → SBI SRST falls through to
sbi_exit → final_exit → magic written → harts park.

Status block layout (all u64 little-endian; status_word written LAST
as a publish barrier so the host trusts the rest of the block once
it sees the PARKED magic):
  +0x00 : status word (PARKED magic / 0)
  +0x08 : peers convergence bitmask
  +0x10 : hart 0's `&scratch->next_addr` PA
  +0x18 : hart 0's `&scratch->next_mode` PA
  +0x20 : hart 0's `&scratch->next_arg1` PA
  +0x28 : hart 0's HSM state PA
  +0x30 : CLINT MSIP[0] PA

Status offset 0xE0000 lives in the firmware's reserved range past
OpenSBI's .text/.data/.bss + scratch (typically <300 KiB) and before
the kernel-DTB at +0x100000.

The host's release sequence (Phase 4b, in bhx daemon) becomes:
  1. Re-image kernel + DTB into DRAM via the existing boot_l2cpu path.
  2. Read the metadata fields from the status block.
  3. Write *next_addr_pa = kernel entry PA.
  4. Write *next_mode_pa = PRV_S = 1.
  5. Write *next_arg1_pa = DTB PA.
  6. Write *hsm_state_pa = SBI_HSM_STATE_START_PENDING = 2.
  7. Write *msip_pa = 1 to fire the M-mode software interrupt.
  Hart 0 wakes from sbi_hsm_hart_wait, mainline runs init_warmboot →
  sbi_hart_switch_mode → S-mode entry. New kernel SBI-HSM-starts the
  remaining harts itself.

State quiesce on the SRST-issuing hart (clearing pending CLINT MSIP /
mtimecmp / external IRQs) is already done by sbi_exit's earlier calls
to sbi_timer_exit / sbi_ipi_exit / sbi_irqchip_exit; we don't
duplicate that here.

A new helper sbi_hsm_hart_state_addr() in sbi_hsm.c exposes the PA
of a hart's HSM state atomic_t, since the file-static
`hart_data_offset` isn't otherwise reachable from platform.c.

